<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maurice, Prince of Orange</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1567–<a href="/years/1625">1625</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Maurice of Nassau · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-orange-nassau">House of Orange-Nassau</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Maurice of Nassau, Prince of Orange from 1618, was stadtholder of Holland and Zeeland from 1585 and later of most other provinces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>. A son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-silent">William the Silent</a>, he reformed the army with his cousin William Louis, introducing regular drill, smaller units and volley fire, and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-stevin">Simon Stevin</a> he recaptured many towns from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in the 1590s. He defeated a Spanish army at Nieuwpoort in 1600 and in 1619 had Oldenbarnevelt executed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maurice-prince-of-orange.jpg" alt="Maurice, Prince of Orange" width="320" height="432" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: School of Michiel Jansz. van Mierevelt · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:School_of_Michiel_Jansz._van_Mierevelt_001.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Maurice was born on 14 November 1567 at the castle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dillenburg">Dillenburg</a>, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-silent">William the Silent</a> and Anna of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, and was named after his maternal grandfather, the Elector Maurice of Saxony. He was raised in Dillenburg by his uncle Johan of Nassau and studied with his cousin Willem Lodewijk at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a> and then at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leiden">Leiden</a>, where he met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-stevin">Simon Stevin</a>. The States of Holland and Zeeland paid for his studies. When his father was murdered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delft">Delft</a> in 1584 he was sixteen. The monarchs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> had refused <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> provinces, which left him the only acceptable candidate for his father's office.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Maurice became stadtholder of Holland and Zeeland in 1585, of Guelders, Overijssel and Utrecht in 1590 and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/groningen">Groningen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drenthe">Drenthe</a> in 1620. In 1587 he was appointed captain-general of the army, bypassing the Earl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leicester">Leicester</a>. With Willem Lodewijk he reorganised the Dutch States Army, and paid close attention to Stevin's siege theories. In the period known as the Ten Glory Years, 1588 to 1598, he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/breda">Breda</a> in 1590, Zutphen and Knodsenburg in 1591, Steenwijk and Coevorden in 1592, Geertruidenberg in 1593 and Groningen in 1594, and in 1597 a further series of towns. These victories rounded out the borders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>.</p><p>At Turnhout in 1597 and Nieuwpoort in 1600 he won pitched battles through cooperation between the arms, with cavalry playing a major part. Drill and exercise, for him, were the fundamental postulates of tactics; volley fire was first used in European combat at Nieuwpoort. Historians note his preference for sieges, and Spanish forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambrogio-spinola">Ambrogio Spinola</a> defeated him at Mülheim in October 1605.</p><p>Maurice began as the protégé of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johan-van-oldenbarnevelt">Johan van Oldenbarnevelt</a>, but they quarrelled over the Twelve Years' Truce of 1609 to 1621, which Oldenbarnevelt signed against his advice, and over religion, Maurice backing the Gomarists and Oldenbarnevelt the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/remonstrants">Remonstrants</a>. Maurice had him arrested and tried, and he was beheaded in 1619 for high treason despite requests for mercy. In 1618 Maurice succeeded his half-brother Philip William as Prince of Orange.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>War with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> resumed in 1621. Maurice died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a> on 23 April 1625, with the Spanish siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/breda">Breda</a> still under way, and Justin of Nassau surrendered the town in June. He had no legitimate children and was succeeded by his half-brother Frederick Henry. His reforms were applied later by pupils such as Marshal Turenne and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustavus-adolphus">Gustavus Adolphus</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>. The island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritius">Mauritius</a> was named in his honour by Wybrant Warwijck in 1598.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Maurice, Prince of Orange” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Maurice of Nassau</b>, stadtholder from 1585, and his cousin William Louis reformed the army with regular pay, smaller units, constant drill and volley fire, and with the engineer Simon Stevin they recaptured towns such as Breda in 1590 and won the Battle of Nieuwpoort in 1600.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 1590s <b>Maurice of Nassau</b> and his cousin William Louis, commanding the forces of the Dutch Republic in its war against Spain, studied the military manuals of classical antiquity and drilled their musketeers to fire by ranks: each rank fired and then withdrew to reload while the…</blockquote><a href="/essays/the-military-revolution">The Military Revolution</a></li><li><blockquote>He had clashed with Stadtholder <b>Maurice of Nassau</b> amid the conflict between Remonstrants and Counter-Remonstrants.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-13">13 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Dutch sailors named Mauritius after Prince <b>Maurice of Nassau</b> in 1598, and in 1638 the Dutch East India Company (VOC) founded Fort Frederik Hendrik at Grand Port to forestall French and English rivals.</blockquote><a href="/state/dutch-mauritius">Dutch Mauritius</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Maurice of Nassau</b> besieged and took the city in 1594, and it joined the Dutch Republic as a province.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-groningen">Lordship of Groningen</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dillenburg">Dillenburg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/netherlands">the Netherlands</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.73,8.28&amp;year=1604" aria-label="Dillenburg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4739 403 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Maurice, Prince of Orange"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5189 673h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5189 673h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.73,8.28&amp;year=1604">What was here in 1604? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
